WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.290
I know there are going to be people who try
to claim that Coraline isn’t a horror movie,

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.700
and therefore I can’t cover it here, on
my horror channel.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.040
And I’d just like to remind those people,
before we get started here, of a few of the

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.750
things that happen in Coraline.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:11.760
Roll the clips.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.880
"AHHHHH!"

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:15.800
"Forever..."

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:18.720
"HA-HA!!!

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.580
AGHHH!!"

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:22.570
Yeah.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:23.570
Horror movie.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.950
There are also references to a couple of the
most well known horror movies of all time,

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.520
so stick around to the end of this video if
you want to hear about them.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.620
[INTRO]

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.880
Before we get into the truly creepy stuff,
there are a few Things You Missed at the beginning

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.320
of the movie, where the filmmakers just couldn’t
help but calling attention to themselves.

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:43.420
Coraline’s family is moving into their new
home with the help of the Ranft Brothers moving

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.180
company, and we end up seeing the Ranft Brothers,
whose name tags read Jerome and Joe.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:50.860
This is a reference to the real life Ranft
Brothers, who are a pair of character sculptors

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.640
in many great animated films, and while they
didn’t sculpt the characters in Coraline,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.450
they did work on director Henry Selick’s
previous stop motion film James and the Giant

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.450
Peach.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.410
So naturally, they’ve graffitied the words
StopMo Rulez on their own truck.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:06.029
And when Joe receives a tip for his moving
services, the photo of George Washington on

00:01:06.029 --> 00:01:09.119
the bill has been replaced by that of the
director, Henry Selick.

00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:12.610
The moving truck is also green, and we first
see it pulled up next to the house, which

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.610
is covered in pink.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.409
In the novella, one of the things that Coraline
finds off-putting about the other world beyond

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:21.060
the door is the way her other bedroom is painted
in shades of green and pink.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.159
But the movie does a great job of showing
us how Coraline found her world to be drury

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:26.630
and boring in ways that the novella was not
able to.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.939
Take these two scenes in the garden for example,
where the real world is made to look damp

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:33.170
and devoid of color while the other world
is bursting with life and colors.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.069
The film also introduces details that the
novella lacked, like the fact that her parents

00:01:37.069 --> 00:01:40.500
in the real world are gardners, or at least
that they write about gardening, and her other

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.450
mother is eventually revealed to be a predatory
insect like creature; insects are the natural

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:45.460
enemy of gardeners.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.189
Throughout the movie you can spot plenty of
clues pointing to this insect motif, and like

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:51.999
her two sets of parents, most of them come
in pairs.

00:01:51.999 --> 00:01:55.929
Examples include the dragonfly pin in Coraline’s
hair and the dragonfly decorations over her

00:01:55.929 --> 00:01:59.609
bed, the mantis tractor that her other father
rides and the mantis picture frame holder

00:01:59.609 --> 00:02:03.349
by Coraline’s bed, the fact that the family
drives a Volkswagen beetle and the chocolate

00:02:03.349 --> 00:02:07.049
beetles that her Other Mother snacks on, and
the picture of the boy in the other drawing

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:11.180
room is in a spider picture frame, and Coraline
ends up fighting the Other Mother on a giant

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.180
spider web.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.680
I also think there’s something to be said
about the fact that the Other Mother created

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.989
this world as a trap to capture Coraline’s
soul.

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:20.670
It’s not a big world, but as one line in
the book puts it, "spiders' webs only have

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.430
to be large enough to catch flies."

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.409
She crafted this world, and tries to trap
Coraline by sewing together the doll as bait,

00:02:26.409 --> 00:02:29.549
and she sews buttons onto the eyes of all
of her victims, so there’s a comparison

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:33.750
to be made there with spiders, who weave their
webs from a string in a very similar manner.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.480
And of course, her final form most closely
resembles a giant spider, and that’s not

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.340
the first time we’ve covered something like
that.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.560
"Ho-ho!!!"

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:44.820
I also found an interesting piece of text
on her real father’s computer screen, right

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.879
before Coraline accidentally turns off the
power.

00:02:46.879 --> 00:02:50.599
If you pause at just the right moment it reads:
“Everything is smelling like success from

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:55.060
out of the cove, a slime covered blob crawls
out from your compost containing *something*

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.060
falls to the ground.”

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.190
It reminds me a lot of the scariest scene
from the book, where Coraline discovers an

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.909
underground cellar, which she comments has
a strange smell, and finds a creature that

00:03:02.909 --> 00:03:06.849
“had almost no features on its face, which
had puffed and swollen like risen bread dough.”

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:09.430
There seem to be a lot of similarities between
these two descriptions.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:13.080
We soon find out that The Other Mother is
in control of everything in the other world.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:14.520
She’s referred to as a Beldam....

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:20.440
"And shush, for the Beldam might be listening..."

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.920
....which traces back to an old French word
meaning old woman or witch.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.159
Coraline has to fight witchcraft with witchcraft.

00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:29.739
She uses this stone, which is known in our
world as an adder stone or witch stone to

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:32.989
locate the ghost children’s eyes, and at
the beginning of the movie, she uses a branch

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:36.730
of poison oak as a dowsing rod, and her neighbor
Wybee accuses her of witchcraft.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.019
"I heard about water witching before but it
doesn't make sense.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:41.909
I mean it's just an ordinary branch."

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:45.349
We’re first given the idea that the Beldam
is in control when Coraline meets her Other

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:49.480
Father, and he plays her a song on the piano,
but the piano is the one controlling him,

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.719
not the other way around.

00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:54.469
It does this by taking control of his hands,
and the Other Mother’s hands being in control

00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:57.280
becomes another motif seen time and time again
in the movie.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.090
The reason for this is because in the novella,
when Coraline and Other Mother make the wager,

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.629
Other Mother agrees to let Coraline go if
she wins, and swears on her mother’s grave

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:05.629
that she’ll keep her word.

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:08.980
But that’s not good enough for Coraline,
so she also swears on her right hand.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:11.360
And there are a ton of references to that
hand in the movie.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.010
When Coraline discovers the other world’s
garden, Other Father has this to say.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.200
“Mother said you’d like it.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:21.169
She knows you like the back of her hand.”

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:24.340
There are also several close ups of Other
Mother’s hands throughout the movie, even

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.819
before her hand is detached.

00:04:25.819 --> 00:04:29.590
We also see several other images that resemble
the true form of the Other Mother’s hands,

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:33.540
like the lightning strike outside the window,
clouds that part to reveal the moon after

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.639
her defeat, and the tea leaves that Ms. Spinks
interpret as a sign of danger in Coraline’s

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:38.639
tea.

00:04:38.639 --> 00:04:42.150
On the contrary, Ms. Forcible reads the tea
leaves upside down, and sees them as a giraffe,

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.289
which may be a clue about her eventually becoming
trapped in the other world, where there’s

00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:47.440
a giraffe toy in Coraline’s room.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.440
Or maybe it’s actually a clue about the
true form of her enemy whose neck extends

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.110
like that of a giraffe.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:56.370
Or maybe it’s actually a clue about where
Coraline’s parents are hidden, in the snow

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.580
globe from the zoo, because giraffes are an
animal you find at the zoo, I don’t know.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.970
It’s gotta be one of those, OK?

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:04.430
Coraline’s main quest in the movie is to
retrieve the eyes of the three ghost children

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:05.740
and find her parents.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:09.250
Although Coraline never knew that children
had gone missing in that house until later,

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.100
there are some clues early on in the movie.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.180
Actually, the first thing we see is the repurposing
of the doll resembling Mrs. Lovat’s sister.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.570
Then, when Coraline explores the house on
the rainy day, she discovers what she calls

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.100
a painfully boring portrait of the little
boy who seems to be the ghost boy that once

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.100
lived there.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.360
"One boring, blue boy.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.420
In a painfully boring painting."

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.910
And finally, Other Mother has pictures of
three children on her own kitchen wall, two

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.300
girls and one boy, just like the three ghosts.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:41.520
In the book, Coraline’s favorite hobby is
being an explorer, and that’s true in the

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.520
movie as well.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.599
The carpet in the drawing room is an image
of a compass.

00:05:44.599 --> 00:05:47.669
Another difference from the book is in the
clothes that Coraline changes into in the

00:05:47.669 --> 00:05:48.669
other realm.

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:50.699
In the movie it’s a shirt with stars on
it.

00:05:50.699 --> 00:05:53.889
After sending the Other Mother’s hand down
the well, Coraline has the following thought

00:05:53.889 --> 00:05:54.889
about the stars:

00:05:54.889 --> 00:05:58.220
“Someone had once told her that if you look
up at the sky from the bottom of a mineshaft,

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:01.410
even in the brightest daylight, you see a
night sky and stars.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.349
Coraline wondered if the hand could see stars
from where it was.”

00:06:04.349 --> 00:06:08.889
"...supposed to be so deep, if you fell to
the bottom and looked up, you'd see a sky

00:06:08.889 --> 00:06:10.830
full of stars in the middle of the day."

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:13.539
So Coraline wears that shirt while she is
trapped in that other realm.

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:16.639
Her situation is bleak, just like being stuck
at the bottom of a well.

00:06:16.639 --> 00:06:20.319
Now if all of this was not enough horror for
you, let me point out a couple of horror movie

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:22.110
references I found in Coraline.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.699
The first is pretty obscure, but the chair
in Coraline’s other room has the same design

00:06:25.699 --> 00:06:27.659
as Jack Torrance’s chair from The Shining.

00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:30.699
The other can be spotted after she escapes
from the other world, where her father is

00:06:30.699 --> 00:06:35.159
playing with the squid toy on her bed in exactly
the same way the facehugger Xenomorph attacks

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.500
it’s victims in Alien.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:46.160
And if that doesn’t convince you that this
is a horror movie, I don’t know what will.

00:06:46.169 --> 00:06:50.300
So that covers the movie adaptation of Coraline,
but there’s so much more to analyse in this

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.570
story, so coming up on Horror History, I’ll
be covering the scariest details that were

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.849
left out of the movie as I talk about Coraline
Jones and The Other Mother.

00:06:56.849 --> 00:07:00.230
You don’t want to miss out on that so make
sure you’re subscribed to CZsWorld for new

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:04.389
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for
notifications and I’ll see you in the next

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:05.389
one.

00:07:05.389 --> 00:07:07.780
Assuming we both survive.

